=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND9 named daemon on `srv1` (10.5.0.4), which serves as the campus-wide recursive DNS resolver for all hosts including Office A clients (PC1, PC2, PC3).

**Mechanism:** `srv1`'s `/etc/bind/named.conf` contains the line `recursion no;`. This directive instructs BIND to operate exclusively as an authoritative server — it will answer queries only for zones it owns (`jeremysitlab.com`). For any external hostname (e.g., google.com, any public domain), BIND immediately returns `REFUSED` with the flag "recursion requested but not available." Since web browsers rely on DNS to translate hostnames to IPs before opening a TCP connection, they receive no address, the connection never starts, and the page times out. Direct-IP ICMP traffic (e.g., `ping 203.0.113.1`) bypasses DNS entirely and succeeds, which is why pings to the ISP gateway work while browsing does not.

**Localization:** Fault is entirely on `srv1` (10.5.0.4), specifically in `/etc/bind/named.conf`, options block. All three campus hosts point to this single nameserver (`nameserver 10.5.0.4` in each `/etc/resolv.conf`), so all offices are affected, though the ticket came from Office A.

**Root cause:** The `recursion no;` directive was introduced into `srv1`'s named.conf, converting the campus resolver from a recursive forwarder into an authoritative-only server, blocking external DNS resolution for all campus clients.

**Fix:** On `srv1`, edit `/etc/bind/named.conf`, remove or replace `recursion no;` with `recursion yes;`, and add a `forwarders { <upstream-dns-ip>; };` entry pointing to the ISP gateway DNS (e.g., 203.0.113.1 on ispa) or a reachable resolver. Then reload with `rndc reload` or `systemctl reload bind9`.
=== END ===